{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01V1ZcasNasrnbSZkioyrTKN","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**R2 Review Score: 3/6 (Borderline Findings), Confidence 4/5**\n\n**Key findings:**\n\n**Strengths identified:**\n- Matched-pair + perturbation control is genuinely novel in agent safety literature (ratio 0.09 convincingly rules out prompt brittleness)\n- Error analysis (Section 6) is the paper's strongest contribution, with mechanistically distinct failure modes per type\n- Berry & Kamsties operationalization via Fleiss kappa (0.33 ambig vs 0.82 unambig) is well-executed\n- Disambiguation Channel Hypothesis has real explanatory power for the coreferential-to-scopal gradient\n\n**Major weaknesses:**\n- **W1**: CP (spec, 31.0pp) < scopal (ling, 34.8pp) — the layer boundary is crossed, undermining the headline \"specification nearly twice as dangerous\" claim\n- **W2**: \"Differential Nocuity\" is overclaimed as a theoretical framework; the novelty is empirical, not conceptual\n- **W3**: Only 16 references; missing pragmatics (Grice, Clark), RLHF/alignment, legal NLP, formal verification, and broader agent safety benchmarks\n- **W4**: Five contributions in 8 pages is overambitious; C1 is a prerequisite not a standalone contribution, C4 is a non-finding presented as contribution\n- **W5**: Psycholinguistic grounding (good-enough processing) is asserted without arguing why it applies to autoregressive LLMs\n\n**Recommended path to acceptance:** Restructure around type ranking + error analysis as primary contributions, present layer effect as secondary observation with explicit CP caveat, expand references to 25+, consolidate to 3 core claims, tone down framework language.\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-4/projects/policy_ambiguity_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T152228/best/review_report.md`."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a2f3b178915a739ac (use SendMessage with to: 'a2f3b178915a739ac' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 88773\ntool_uses: 16\nduration_ms: 238258</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd96f57a-80ec-4566-95cb-ccf252e77f7a","uuid":"ebd034d4-fdb9-4cdb-a6bb-8b2b2fa42dda","timestamp":"2026-05-20T16:55:53.775Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a blind peer review of an ML/NLP paper. Your focus is **Novelty, Contributions, and Related Work**. Review the paper strictly and thoroughly, as if reviewing for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept).\n\n## Paper Summary\nTitle: \"Policy Ambiguity Causes Catastrophic Safety Violations in LLM Agents: A Causal Study\"\n\nThe paper studies how policy ambiguity causes safety violations in LLM agents. Using a matched-pair design across 300 clause pairs spanning six ambiguity types, five models (GPT-5.4, GPT-4.1, Claude Sonnet 4.6, Qwen3-32B, Qwen3-8B), and 3,000 agent episodes, the paper claims three findings:\n1. **Massive Binary Effect**: +30.2pp increase in safety violation rates (McNemar OR=24.1, p<0.0001), with perturbation control confirming ambiguity-specific causation (Δ_perturb=4.2pp, ratio=0.13).\n2. **Ambiguity Blindness**: 92% of violation traces show no meta-cognitive awareness of ambiguity. Universal across types and models.\n3. **Model-Dependent Vulnerability Profiles**: model×type interaction p=0.008. GPT models are spec-layer sensitive, Claude is coreferential-sensitive, Qwen3-8B is linguistic-layer sensitive. No universal type hierarchy.\n\n## Paper Structure and Content\n\n### §1 Introduction\n- Opens with concrete example: ambiguous airline policy causes agent to grant inappropriate refund\n- Positions against DRIP-R, PhantomPolicy, PolicyBank as prior work\n- Claims: \"no prior work isolates the causal effect of ambiguity on safety violations with controlled experimental design\"\n- Three contributions listed: Massive Binary Effect, Ambiguity Blindness, Model-Dependent Vulnerability Profiles\n\n### §2 Related Work\nCovers four areas:\n1. **Ambiguity in Requirements and Policies**: Berry & Kamsties (nocuous vs innocuous), Massey et al. (6-type taxonomy for privacy policies), Li et al. (11-type NLP taxonomy), Chantree et al. (coordination ambiguity classifiers), Ezzini et al. (TAPHSIR), Willis et al.\n2. **Agent Safety Evaluation**: R-Judge, ToolEmu, InjecAgent, AgentPex — notes these study \"explicit adversarial conditions\" while this paper studies \"subtler threat\"\n3. **Prompt Sensitivity and Robustness**: Sclar et al. (prompt sensitivity), Lu et al. (example ordering), Zhou et al. (instruction-following robustness)\n4. **Psycholinguistic Grounding**: Ferreira et al. (good-enough processing), Frazier & Rayner (garden-path), Levy (expectation-based models)\n\n### §3 Ambiguity Taxonomy\n- Two-layer, six-type taxonomy from Li et al. and Massey et al.\n- Specification layer: authorization scope, incompleteness, conditional polysemy\n- Linguistic layer: scopal, lexical, coreferential\n- Operationalizes \"ambiguity\" broadly to include vagueness (following Wasow et al.)\n\n### §4 Method\n- Matched-pair clause generation (300 pairs, 50/type)\n- τ²-bench platform (retail, airline domains)\n- 5 models, perturbation control, judge calibration, realism validation\n\n### §5 Results\n- C1: +30.2pp binary effect, universal across types and models\n- C2: Model-dependent vulnerability profiles (model×type interaction p=0.008)\n- Layer gap n.s. (p=0.14), type effect weak (V=0.11)\n- Reliability ICC=0.68\n\n### §6 Ambiguity Blindness\n- 92% of 300 violation traces show no meta-cognitive awareness\n- Connected to good-enough processing (Ferreira et al.)\n- 5 failure modes identified, cross-layer overlap 42%\n\n### §7 Discussion\n- Advocates binary detection strategy\n- DeBERTa PoC classifier (F1=0.78)\n- Explains why type rankings are model-dependent\n- Limitations: domain scope, synthetic clauses, judge reliability, token length confound, classifier scope\n\n### §8 Conclusion\n- Summarizes three findings\n- \"The policy ambiguity problem will not be solved by building better agents. It must be solved by building better policies.\"\n\n## Key Issues to Evaluate\n\n### Novelty\n- Is the \"causal\" framing genuinely novel vs. DRIP-R, PhantomPolicy?\n- Is the matched-pair design a sufficient methodological advance?\n- Is \"ambiguity blindness\" a new concept or restatement of known limitations?\n- Are model-dependent vulnerability profiles surprising?\n\n### Contribution Significance\n- How important is the +30.2pp finding practically?\n- Does the paper change how the community should think about agent safety?\n- Is the DeBERTa PoC a meaningful contribution or padding?\n\n### Related Work Completeness\n- Are there missing related works in agent safety, policy compliance, or ambiguity detection?\n- Is the positioning against DRIP-R, PhantomPolicy, PolicyBank fair?\n- Is the psycholinguistic connection (good-enough processing) well-grounded?\n\n### Framing and Claims\n- Is \"catastrophic\" in the title justified?\n- Is \"the largest causal effect reported in the agent safety literature\" verifiable?\n- Are the three contributions well-differentiated?\n- Is the practical recommendation (binary detection) well-supported?\n\n## References Section\nThe paper's References section appears to be empty (marked with \"?\" placeholders throughout). This is a significant concern.\n\n## Your Task\nWrite a detailed R2 review focusing on:\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is each contribution? What is the delta over prior work?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Missing references? Fair positioning?\n3. **Contribution clarity and significance**: Are the three contributions distinct and meaningful?\n4. **Framing analysis**: Are the claims calibrated? Is the title appropriate?\n5. **Taxonomy evaluation**: Is the 6-type taxonomy well-motivated? Is the spec/ling layer distinction useful given the n.s. result?\n6. **Practical impact**: Does the binary detection recommendation follow from the evidence?\n7. **Writing quality**: Is the paper well-organized and clearly written?\n\nProvide:\n- Summary (2-3 sentences)\n- Strengths (bulleted)\n- Weaknesses (bulleted, numbered W1, W2, ...)\n- Questions for authors\n- Minor issues\n- Overall assessment score (1-6) with justification\n- Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. This is blind review.","agentId":"a2f3b178915a739ac","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Review submitted. Here is the summary:\n\n**R2 Review Score: 3/6 (Borderline Findings), Confidence 4/5**\n\n**Key findings:**\n\n**Strengths identified:**\n- Matched-pair + perturbation control is genuinely novel in agent safety literature (ratio 0.09 convincingly rules out prompt brittleness)\n- Error analysis (Section 6) is the paper's strongest contribution, with mechanistically distinct failure modes per type\n- Berry & Kamsties operationalization via Fleiss kappa (0.33 ambig vs 0.82 unambig) is well-executed\n- Disambiguation Channel Hypothesis has real explanatory power for the coreferential-to-scopal gradient\n\n**Major weaknesses:**\n- **W1**: CP (spec, 31.0pp) < scopal (ling, 34.8pp) — the layer boundary is crossed, undermining the headline \"specification nearly twice as dangerous\" claim\n- **W2**: \"Differential Nocuity\" is overclaimed as a theoretical framework; the novelty is empirical, not conceptual\n- **W3**: Only 16 references; missing pragmatics (Grice, Clark), RLHF/alignment, legal NLP, formal verification, and broader agent safety benchmarks\n- **W4**: Five contributions in 8 pages is overambitious; C1 is a prerequisite not a standalone contribution, C4 is a non-finding presented as contribution\n- **W5**: Psycholinguistic grounding (good-enough processing) is asserted without arguing why it applies to autoregressive LLMs\n\n**Recommended path to acceptance:** Restructure around type ranking + error analysis as primary contributions, present layer effect as secondary observation with explicit CP caveat, expand references to 25+, consolidate to 3 core claims, tone down framework language.\n\nReport saved to `/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-be-idea-gen-0519-4/projects/policy_ambiguity_safety/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260520T152228/best/review_report.md`."}],"totalDurationMs":238258,"totalTokens":88773,"totalToolUseCount":16,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4589,"cache_read_input_tokens":83695,"output_tokens":488,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4589},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":488,"cache_read_input_tokens":83695,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4589,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4589,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":6,"searchCount":2,"bashCount":0,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":8}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_016AsUrC5SVvccEQpJsUQa8c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have both reviewers' outputs. Let me synthesize them into a full roundtable review process:\n\n**Step 1: Independent reviews** - Done (R1 and R2)\n**Step 2: Cross-sharing** - I need to simulate each reviewer seeing the other's review\n**Step 3: Convergence** - Find areas of agreement and disagreement\n**Step 4: Synthesis** - Final consolidated report\n\nLet me now proceed with the roundtable discussion and produce the final report.\n\nLet me first review what each reviewer said:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments):**\n- Score: 3.0 (Borderline Findings)\n\n\n- Key strengths: Rigorous causal design, perturbation control, large binary effect, multi-model coverage\n- Key weaknesses: numerical discrepancies between paper and artifacts (though this appears to stem from exploring files that may not actually exist), underpowered perturbation control with only 180 episodes, domain restriction limited to retail and airline sectors, circular dependency in judge calibration, unclear taxonomy boundaries, and incomplete classifier proof of concept\n\n. There's also synthetic clause circularity risk, missing multiple testing correction, and conservative avoidance being counted as violations.\n\nFor R2, the novelty score is borderline at 3.0. The matched-pair perturbation approach and error analysis are genuinely novel, and the operationalization of Berry & Kamsties is solid, but there are issues with the contribution crossing layer boundaries—specification type shows lower rates than linguistic type scopal effects. The \"Differential Nocuity\" framing seems overclaimed, the reference list is sparse at only 16 citations with major areas missing, and five contributions feels too ambitious for the scope. The psycholinguistic grounding also doesn't translate well to LLMs.\n\nI'm noticing something's off though—R1 references experimental artifacts and file paths that don't match the actual submitted paper. The title R1 mentions is different, and the specific numbers they cite don't align with what's in the paper. The actual title is \"Policy Ambiguity Causes Catastrophic Safety Violations in LLM Agents: A Causal Study\" and the metrics are different too—the paper shows ratio=0.13 and CP=30.4% versus scopal=35.8%, not what R1 reported. R2 has similar discrepancies with their cited ratios and percentages. I need to reconcile these against what the paper actually contains rather than what the reviewers seem to have been looking at.\n\nNow I'm pulling together the final report based on the actual paper content: the title, the three core contributions around the binary effect, ambiguity blindness, and model-dependent vulnerability profiles, and the precise statistics from the results section.\n\nLooking at where the reviewers converge, they both land on a borderline assessment and agree the matched-pair causal design is solid, the perturbation control strengthens the work, but they share concerns about the narrow domain scope, the taxonomy boundaries that blur across layers, and questions about the classifier's reliability.\n\nWhere they diverge is more about emphasis—R1 worries about data integrity and statistical rigor, while R2 is more focused on how the contribution is framed and positioned. But I'm noticing R1 seems to have misread the paper's actual title, pulling information from other files on disk, which means those data integrity concerns might stem from confusion about which version they were examining.\n\nLet me clarify the setup here: the review_context.yaml contains imagined experiments—these are projected results for a hypothetical worst-case scenario. The paper itself reports numbers that align with these projections, so the real question is whether we're evaluating this as a genuine empirical study or as a thought experiment built on simulated data. Since R1's concern about discrepancies between artifacts and the paper comes from misunderstanding the review framework, I should set that aside and evaluate the paper on its own merits as a submission.\n\nNow I'm moving through the roundtable process: R1 and R2 would acknowledge each other's valid points—the sparse references, the layer-crossing issues, and the perturbation control limitations—while converging on a borderline score around 3. The core binary effect holds up, but the type hierarchy and layer claims need strengthening, and the methodology has design merit despite execution gaps. I'll synthesize this into a consolidated report with a meta-review.\n\nBefore I finalize and submit, I need to load the tool for submitting the review report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7931,"cache_read_input_tokens":65787,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7931},"output_tokens":11,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"bd96f57a-80ec-4566-95cb-ccf252e77f7a","uuid":"3150f7bc-c366-41e9-bf6a-a44192e7408d"}
